Okay, I've just finished a series of edits to improve the lighting and material of the tin mouse model. I added the RoomEnvironment for image‑based lighting, tweaked the tin material color and metalness, and updated the import paths to include the three.js addons. The capture script has already run and produced dailies for each key beat. I’ve looked at the screenshots for shots 5 (under the bed) and 6 (window‑light crossing) and they look good, so the lighting changes are effective.

Now I need to turn my attention to the geometry of the mouse model itself, specifically the ears. In the earlier version of main.js, the ear is created like this:

```js
const ear = cast(new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.024, 0.024, 0.006, 16),
    tinPink));
ear.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2.4;
ear.position.set(ex, 0.1, 0.045);
```

From the dailies, the ears look a bit too large and their orientation is slightly off—they seem to stick out too far and don’t follow the curvature of the head naturally. Also, the ears currently only have a rotation about the X axis; adding a slight rotation about Z could give them a more natural tilt as they swing with the head movement (the variable `ex` controls the head’s rotation, so we can tie the ear tilt to that).

I want to make the ears a bit slimmer, reduce the radius from 0.024 to about 0.017 while keeping the height the same (0.006). That will give a more delicate look appropriate for a tin mouse. Then I’ll adjust the X rotation from `Math.PI / 2.4` to a slightly larger angle (`Math.PI / 2.6`) so the ears tilt a bit more forward. Finally, I’ll add a Z rotation proportional to `ex` (e.g., `ear.rotation.z = ex * 6`) to make the ears follow the head’s swing. The position also needs a tiny tweak: lower the Y coordinate a bit (from 0.1 to 0.092) and move the Z coordinate slightly inward (from 0.045 to 0.04) to keep the ear anchored correctly after the geometry change.

All of these changes are localized to the ear definition, so I can perform a targeted edit in main.js, replacing the exact block of code that defines the ear. I’ll use the Edit tool with `replace_all: false` to avoid unintended replacements elsewhere. This edit should improve the visual fidelity of the ears in the upcoming shots, especially the later ones where the mouse is more animated.

